1
00:00:09,196 --> 00:00:14,997
So last time we talked about QoS and
we talked about classification,

2
00:00:14,997 --> 00:00:20,396
shaping, and scheduling, and
how they all fit together to form

3
00:00:20,396 --> 00:00:26,607
the ability to put some sort of quality
of service properties on traffic.

4
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:31,771
So here we're going to talk about
the Linux utility that's available to

5
00:00:31,771 --> 00:00:37,388
be able to do all of those QoS things and
it's called tc for traffic control.

6
00:00:40,823 --> 00:00:45,076
So tc is used for QoS setup, but

7
00:00:45,076 --> 00:00:49,174
it can actually do much more.

8
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:54,117
We'll hint at some of the additional
things that you can do with it,

9
00:00:54,117 --> 00:00:57,164
but we'll leave that for you to explore.

10
00:00:57,164 --> 00:01:00,216
So what we're going to do here is really,

11
00:01:00,216 --> 00:01:05,023
this is a pretty complicated utility,
pretty powerful, but

12
00:01:05,023 --> 00:01:09,924
once you kind of start understanding
the key constructs in it,

13
00:01:09,924 --> 00:01:14,369
it becomes something that you
can more easily work with.

14
00:01:14,369 --> 00:01:20,588
So the key constructs are the qdisc,
the class, and filter.

15
00:01:20,588 --> 00:01:23,721
So we'll go through each of those and

16
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:28,686
talk about how you specify them and
what their purpose is.

17
00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:36,025
So the queuing discipline or qdisc is the
main object for shaping and scheduling.

18
00:01:36,025 --> 00:01:38,758
So it kind of groups them together.

19
00:01:38,758 --> 00:01:42,366
We have them as separate things, but
it kind of groups them together.

20
00:01:42,366 --> 00:01:46,655
So this is where you put in
the properties onto the traffic.

21
00:01:46,655 --> 00:01:52,499
So every interface must have
an ingress and egress qdisc.

22
00:01:52,499 --> 00:01:56,493
So what these are is
packets go into a qdisc and

23
00:01:56,493 --> 00:02:01,331
then they go out the other
side according to some policy

24
00:02:01,331 --> 00:02:06,499
based on whatever queuing
discipline that you inserted.

25
00:02:06,499 --> 00:02:13,378
So there's a simple example is a pfifo,
it's first in, first out.

26
00:02:13,378 --> 00:02:18,140
So this is the default one used by
Linux if you don't configure anything.

27
00:02:20,829 --> 00:02:26,305
So how do you go about adding and
removing a qdisc?

28
00:02:26,305 --> 00:02:30,521
So there's a lot to
the individual command.

29
00:02:30,521 --> 00:02:35,656
So we're breaking it down into
its individual pieces and

30
00:02:35,656 --> 00:02:40,264
then we're going to build it
up as an example as we go.

31
00:02:40,264 --> 00:02:46,749
So the first part is much like
with all of the other utilities,

32
00:02:46,749 --> 00:02:51,752
it starts with the command name tc,
all right?

33
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:57,251
Then here we're specifying that we're
going to want to apply this to a qdisc.

34
00:02:57,251 --> 00:03:02,819
So this particular construct
that's important in this case.

35
00:03:02,819 --> 00:03:07,985
Then we can do stuff like add,
delete, replace,

36
00:03:07,985 --> 00:03:16,363
and you apply it to whatever device you're
applying it to, so you specify that.

37
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:21,652
So as an example we're
going to say tc qdisc

38
00:03:21,652 --> 00:03:26,328
add to device, eth0, all right?

39
00:03:26,328 --> 00:03:31,264
So, we've said each qdisc must be
associated with the device and

40
00:03:31,264 --> 00:03:35,416
this allows us to add, and
delete, and remove qdisc.

41
00:03:35,416 --> 00:03:40,817
The next part of the command
says parent qdisc-id or

42
00:03:40,817 --> 00:03:44,883
root, followed by handle qdisc-id.

43
00:03:44,883 --> 00:03:48,672
So qdiscs can be hierarchical.

44
00:03:48,672 --> 00:03:52,209
So you need to specify the parent or

45
00:03:52,209 --> 00:03:56,484
root if this is the root of the hierarchy.

46
00:03:56,484 --> 00:04:00,961
So it doesn't necessarily mean that they
have to have children, but they could.

47
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:05,457
And so if this is the top level one,
it would be considered the root.

48
00:04:05,457 --> 00:04:08,884
And then you can also specify a handle.

49
00:04:08,884 --> 00:04:14,400
And so this is something that would
be referenced by the children.

50
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:19,286
All right, so here we're adding to this,
we're saying root,

51
00:04:19,286 --> 00:04:24,078
since this is the root of the hierarchy
and we're going to specify

52
00:04:24,078 --> 00:04:28,610
a handle of 1:, so
it's an implied kind of number there.

53
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:32,119
But this is kind of what
you see in examples.

54
00:04:32,119 --> 00:04:35,956
So this is something
now we can refer to for

55
00:04:35,956 --> 00:04:40,902
future commands if we want to
refer to this as a parent.

56
00:04:40,902 --> 00:04:49,195
All right, so the next part of
the command we would say the qdisc.

57
00:04:49,195 --> 00:04:53,724
So this is the type and we have
some examples in the coming slides.

58
00:04:53,724 --> 00:04:59,454
And then the qdisc specific parameters,
so that's all of what's here.

59
00:04:59,454 --> 00:05:02,300
So this first one is the qdisc.

60
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:07,505
And so this one refers to tbf or
token bucket filter.

61
00:05:07,505 --> 00:05:11,090
And then the parameters we
would have to go look up for

62
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:14,689
the token bucket filter,
what are its parameters?

63
00:05:14,689 --> 00:05:17,481
And it allows you to have a rate so

64
00:05:17,481 --> 00:05:21,633
I could specify that I want
it to be at this rate.

65
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:24,928
I could specify the burst size,

66
00:05:24,928 --> 00:05:29,817
I could specify a latency if I want,
all right?

67
00:05:29,817 --> 00:05:34,165
And so there's other ones
that we'll see as an example.

68
00:05:34,165 --> 00:05:39,002
So this all right here forms a single,

69
00:05:39,002 --> 00:05:42,183
creates a single qdisc.

70
00:05:42,183 --> 00:05:49,006
So some example qdisc,
we talked about a simple packet fifo or

71
00:05:49,006 --> 00:05:55,583
a byte fifo, I believe and
it's just first in, first out.

72
00:05:55,583 --> 00:05:59,883
The one we showed earlier,
this pfifo fast, well,

73
00:05:59,883 --> 00:06:04,477
this is looking at based on
some type of service fields,

74
00:06:04,477 --> 00:06:09,582
classifying it into different
queues internal to the qdisc.

75
00:06:09,582 --> 00:06:14,555
And then it'll transmit packets in

76
00:06:14,555 --> 00:06:19,031
kind of a prioritized first in,

77
00:06:19,031 --> 00:06:23,190
first out order, all right?

78
00:06:23,190 --> 00:06:30,382
Another SFQ stochastic fair queuing,
right?

79
00:06:30,382 --> 00:06:37,279
So this is applying a fair queuing
algorithm to apply to different queues and

80
00:06:37,279 --> 00:06:43,122
then use a round robin algorithm
to transmit from those queues.

81
00:06:43,122 --> 00:06:46,529
Again, this is all internal to one qdisc.

82
00:06:46,529 --> 00:06:50,669
And so this is not like we're going to do,

83
00:06:50,669 --> 00:06:55,572
specify the classification and
then do that.

84
00:06:55,572 --> 00:07:02,708
All right, so that's that algorithm and
you can go look up more about it.

85
00:07:02,708 --> 00:07:06,445
Token bucket filter, so this is TBF and

86
00:07:06,445 --> 00:07:10,976
this is kind of what we
learned about last time,

87
00:07:10,976 --> 00:07:17,122
where tokens are kind of entered
into a bucket at a specific rate.

88
00:07:17,122 --> 00:07:22,630
And then you have a bucket size
to determine the burst size,

89
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:27,822
and then it's just packets
would conform at that rate.

90
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:30,866
So those are just some examples.

91
00:07:30,866 --> 00:07:35,902
Now, qdiscs can be classless or classful,

92
00:07:35,902 --> 00:07:42,707
so examples on the previous
two slides were all classless,

93
00:07:42,707 --> 00:07:46,805
so it just applies without a class.

94
00:07:46,805 --> 00:07:53,155
Some qdiscs can contain classes which
contain further qdiscs, so traffic

95
00:07:53,155 --> 00:07:59,821
may then be enqueued in any number of
inner qdisc which are within the classes.

96
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:05,299
So we got another command
where we can add a class,

97
00:08:05,299 --> 00:08:09,720
right, and
we'll walk through this as well.

98
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:14,231
But first,
as an illustration of this here,

99
00:08:14,231 --> 00:08:21,160
we might have a qdisc and
this is the hierarchical token bucket.

100
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:28,828
So we're going to apply some filters to it
which is put it into different classes and

101
00:08:28,828 --> 00:08:34,030
then those classes then feed
into qdiscs themselves.

102
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:40,239
All right, so
this is when we have a class full qdisc,

103
00:08:40,239 --> 00:08:44,180
like hierarchical token bucket.

104
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:50,840
This is when we would have these things,
these classes, all right?

105
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:54,896
And so
you can see there's other ones like pfifo.

106
00:08:54,896 --> 00:09:02,608
Fast, is not classful,
it's a classless qdisc.

107
00:09:02,608 --> 00:09:06,270
And so just whatever goes in, goes out.

108
00:09:06,270 --> 00:09:10,010
There's no kind of parents and
children from that.

109
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:16,510
All right, so
use this picture in the back of your mind.

110
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:23,240
All right, so let's go back to adding and
removing classes.

111
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:29,530
So it's nearly identical to qdisc,
but it can't be root.

112
00:09:29,530 --> 00:09:35,831
Instead, you have a class-id instead of
a handle, but it's the same basic type and

113
00:09:35,831 --> 00:09:40,019
the type(qdisc) must be
the same as the parent qdisc.

114
00:09:40,019 --> 00:09:42,150
All right, so let's look at an example.

115
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:51,328
So this is an example of a classful qdisc,
the hierarchical token bucket.

116
00:09:51,328 --> 00:09:57,065
So let's say, we have a token bucket
where we want to have different

117
00:09:57,065 --> 00:10:04,100
classes of traffic, where we want to
start rate limiting in different manners.

118
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:10,066
I want to rate limit some of the traffic
at 30 and some at 70, right?

119
00:10:10,066 --> 00:10:18,240
And then that feeds into class list qdisc,
fq_codel.

120
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:22,924
All right, so here we would add
the queuing disk, the qdisc.

121
00:10:22,924 --> 00:10:28,820
So qdisc add dev eth0,
is going to be the root.

122
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:31,349
We're going to give it a handle and

123
00:10:31,349 --> 00:10:36,324
we're going to say it's type
hierarchical token bucket, htb.

124
00:10:36,324 --> 00:10:38,938
So now we want to add a class under it.

125
00:10:38,938 --> 00:10:46,338
So maybe the first level,
we're only adding the first class,

126
00:10:46,338 --> 00:10:51,310
so we can say add .tcclass add dev eth0.

127
00:10:51,310 --> 00:10:53,900
So that's that kind of first
part that we looked at.

128
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:59,680
The parent is 99, so that's this 99.

129
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:03,840
The classid is going to be 99:1.

130
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:09,540
And we're going to say we
want it to be the qdisc htb.

131
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:12,971
Remember, it has to match and

132
00:11:12,971 --> 00:11:18,500
we're going to have it a rate of 100Mbit.

133
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:21,840
Then under that we're going to
add these two classes.

134
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:26,040
All right, so
we're going to have these two classes.

135
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:33,560
The parent is both this one, so you can
see the parent is 99:1 + classid, 99:1.

136
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:34,974
All right, so that's that.

137
00:11:34,974 --> 00:11:39,660
And then we give them
each their own classid.

138
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:44,702
And then we're going to say, as part of
this hierarchical token bucket, rate, 30.

139
00:11:44,702 --> 00:11:48,238
So we're dividing that
100 into this 30 and 70.

140
00:11:50,376 --> 00:11:56,978
And then we're going to now
add these qdisc at the end.

141
00:11:56,978 --> 00:12:02,328
So qdisc add dev eth0,
the parent is going to

142
00:12:02,328 --> 00:12:07,940
be 99:10, so that's this one.

143
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:13,158
And then we're going to set
the qdisc to be fq_codal.

144
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:21,310
All right, so now we're not going to
get into hierarchical token bucket,

145
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:27,546
but there's some properties where
the tokens can be shared among these.

146
00:12:27,546 --> 00:12:32,741
So it's a little more than just,
we're dividing them,

147
00:12:32,741 --> 00:12:37,106
the bandwidth equally
among these children.

148
00:12:37,106 --> 00:12:43,680
So that's something that you
can read into on your own.

149
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:49,950
But this is showing how we can use
Linux to set up the q and disc policy.

150
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:56,180
All right, so finally,
let's talk about filters.

151
00:12:56,180 --> 00:13:01,800
These filters are for specifying
which traffic is in which class.

152
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:05,728
So we would have similar command,
tc filter.

153
00:13:05,728 --> 00:13:09,872
So instead of tc qdisc or tc class, again,

154
00:13:09,872 --> 00:13:14,594
add|replace|delete, assign it to a device,

155
00:13:14,594 --> 00:13:19,544
dev eth0, give it its parent,
give it a handle and

156
00:13:19,544 --> 00:13:24,169
the rest of protocol priority and
filter type.

157
00:13:24,169 --> 00:13:25,934
So let's walk through these.

158
00:13:25,934 --> 00:13:31,531
So the top, you need to specify
the device associated with it.

159
00:13:31,531 --> 00:13:35,520
Also you need to specify the parent qdisc.

160
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:41,095
All right, so the next part is specify

161
00:13:41,095 --> 00:13:45,635
the protocol this applies to.

162
00:13:45,635 --> 00:13:50,950
So commonly, you'll just see all,
but you might see ip or

163
00:13:50,950 --> 00:13:56,688
ipv6 for narrowing it down into
specific types of traffic.

164
00:13:56,688 --> 00:14:01,456
And then priority will specify
the order of evaluation.

165
00:14:01,456 --> 00:14:05,320
So a lower priority number
means higher priority.

166
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:11,050
All right, so that's protocol ip prior 1.

167
00:14:12,140 --> 00:14:15,613
And then the last part
is a filter type and

168
00:14:15,613 --> 00:14:19,900
then the filter type specific parameters.

169
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:22,396
And now there's many
different types of filters.

170
00:14:22,396 --> 00:14:25,090
I think on the next slide,
we list out a few of them.

171
00:14:25,090 --> 00:14:28,094
Each of them will have its own man page.

172
00:14:28,094 --> 00:14:31,798
So shown is a basic one, the u32.

173
00:14:31,798 --> 00:14:38,958
So I specify the filter type and
then the u32,

174
00:14:38,958 --> 00:14:44,507
I can say stuff like, match ip src and

175
00:14:44,507 --> 00:14:50,906
then have my action to apply it into this.

176
00:14:50,906 --> 00:14:55,410
Basically, put it into this
particular class, classid 99:10.

177
00:14:57,740 --> 00:15:00,468
All right, so here's examples.

178
00:15:00,468 --> 00:15:05,095
So the u32 is a generic filtering
on arbitrary packet data.

179
00:15:05,095 --> 00:15:08,940
It's assisted by syntax to
abstract common operations.

180
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:15,570
The fw is a filter based on fwmark.

181
00:15:15,570 --> 00:15:22,900
And so it directly maps fwmark values,
sorry, values to traffic classes.

182
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:28,891
And then a really interesting one is
flow or was a related one flower,

183
00:15:28,891 --> 00:15:36,140
which does some flow based classifiers and
filtering packets based on their flow.

184
00:15:38,372 --> 00:15:42,957
All right, so these filters,
as we kind of did point out,

185
00:15:42,957 --> 00:15:47,364
but these filters have a match part and
an action part.

186
00:15:47,364 --> 00:15:52,004
And each has syntax that is
specific to the match condition.

187
00:15:52,004 --> 00:15:56,267
And each has the ability to
specify the action of the form,

188
00:15:56,267 --> 00:16:00,860
either classid,
followed by the actual classid.

189
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:04,257
So the keyword classid,
and then the number,

190
00:16:04,257 --> 00:16:07,056
which pushes the packets that match.

191
00:16:07,056 --> 00:16:14,905
So anything that matches this will
be pushed into this classid, right?

192
00:16:14,905 --> 00:16:17,459
So if you recall,
we created these classes,

193
00:16:17,459 --> 00:16:19,686
we showed how you can create a class.

194
00:16:19,686 --> 00:16:24,500
This is one of the mechanisms so
you can push traffic to it.

195
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:30,244
And then an action is another
thing you can specify.

196
00:16:30,244 --> 00:16:34,440
And this is perform some action
specific to the match filter.

197
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:38,368
So you can look at the man page for that.

198
00:16:38,368 --> 00:16:41,061
We're not really going to go into it, but

199
00:16:41,061 --> 00:16:45,150
that's where a lot of this
additional power of tc comes in.

200
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:51,578
So there's another interesting
qdisc that I'll mention,

201
00:16:51,578 --> 00:16:54,924
it's called NetEm, for network emulation.

202
00:16:54,924 --> 00:16:57,211
So it provides functionality for

203
00:16:57,211 --> 00:17:02,492
testing protocols by emulating
the properties of real-world networks.

204
00:17:02,492 --> 00:17:05,382
So maybe you want to add
delays to packets, or

205
00:17:05,382 --> 00:17:11,320
add packet loss with some probability,
so that's something that NetEm can do.

206
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:15,808
So let's look at an example use of NetEm.

207
00:17:15,808 --> 00:17:20,263
So say I want to create a new application
or protocol, and I want to test how it

208
00:17:20,263 --> 00:17:24,340
would behave under different
conditions on a wireless network.

209
00:17:24,340 --> 00:17:28,093
So I want to say, great,
imagine I had a wireless network,

210
00:17:28,093 --> 00:17:31,630
I have this application,
how would it actually work?

211
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:38,274
So what we can do is we can
insert a Linux node in between.

212
00:17:38,274 --> 00:17:41,762
So you can see this,
this could be a container lab set up.

213
00:17:41,762 --> 00:17:45,720
So here's one node, here's another node,
here's another node.

214
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:46,669
Inside of it,

215
00:17:46,669 --> 00:17:51,980
I would do stuff like use ip route to be
able to forward traffic back and forth.

216
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:58,526
And then I would use tc to
add some properties that is

217
00:17:58,526 --> 00:18:04,017
emulating the wireless network, right?

218
00:18:04,017 --> 00:18:09,101
So here we're replacing,
so tc qdisc replace,

219
00:18:09,101 --> 00:18:13,100
much as we do add device eth0.

220
00:18:13,100 --> 00:18:15,500
This is going to be the root.

221
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:20,303
The qdisc that I'm using is netem,
and then the qdisc

222
00:18:20,303 --> 00:18:25,546
properties are, I want to delay for
100 milliseconds.

223
00:18:32,217 --> 00:18:36,040
With, I don't remember
the exact parameters,

224
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:40,609
with a 12-millisecond,
I think this is variance, and

225
00:18:40,609 --> 00:18:44,915
then at a 10% probability to drop packets,
right?

226
00:18:44,915 --> 00:18:50,028
